  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure remains anchored over the region through all\nlevels of the atmosphere this week, nurturing the ongoing dry\nweather stretch. Main highlights for the next several days will\nthen be monitoring the vast amounts of dry air festering over\nthe area that will bolster afternoon RH values bottoming out\ninto the teens to low 20s along the spine of the Appalachians.\nForecast dew points and minimum RH values are on track to be\naround similar readings observed on Wednesday, but as mentioned\nover the past few forecast cycles the risk for fire spread will\nbe low in response to light column winds. The large influence of\ndry air will also mitigate widespread fog over the next few\ndays, but brief stints of patchy fog confined to the river\nvalleys may be observed around sunrise.\n\nNext opportunity for rain remains on track for the end of the\nweekend once the triumphant ridge breaks down. Two surface\nfeatures, a coastal low and a disturbance tracking across the\ncountry, will take aim for the Central Appalachians on Sunday\nand bring light accumulations back to the forecast. Long range\nmodels show a more auspicious system moving into the region\naround the time that Thanksgiving holiday travel picks up on\nWednesday, bringing widespread rain and wintry precipitation \nalong the mountains. While something to potentially note, there\nis still plenty of time to see how this feature renders out \nwith future forecast cycles over the next week.",
